To reflect on this, it's key to contemplate strategies for identifying prime numbers within a range. While a straightforward approach would involve iterating through numbers less than n and checking for primality, this would be inefficient for large n, as discussed in the reflections on the previous challenge. Instead, we can leverage the Sieve of Eratosthenes, an efficient algorithm for finding all primes up to a specified integer, to pre-compute prime numbers within a manageable range. This pre-computation will enable us to quickly look up the count of primes less than any given n within our constraint, ensuring that the function runs in an expected time.

For the given constraint of n up to 50000, we can pre-compute all prime numbers up to 50000 using the Sieve of Eratosthenes. This approach optimizes the runtime complexity significantly, allowing the function to solve the problem in approximately O(n log log n) due to the nature of the algorithm, which is efficient for large ranges of n.

